@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Tips & Techniques";
}

<h1>@ViewData["Title"]</h1>
<p class="lead">Whether you are just starting out or looking to sharpen your skills, these tips will help you on the mountain.</p>

<div class="row mt-4">
    <div class="col-md-6 mb-4">
        <div class="card h-100">
            <div class="card-header bg-success text-white">
                <h5 class="mb-0">Beginner Tips</h5>
            </div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Take a lesson.</strong> Even one or two lessons from a certified instructor will save you weeks of bad habits.</li>
                    <li><strong>Start on green runs.</strong> Build confidence and control before moving to blues.</li>
                    <li><strong>Lean forward.</strong> Keep your weight over the front of your boots, leaning back causes loss of control.</li>
                    <li><strong>Look ahead, not down.</strong> Focus where you want to go, not at your ski tips.</li>
                    <li><strong>Pizza and French fries.</strong> Use a wedge (pizza) to slow down and parallel skis (French fries) to speed up, the classic beginner technique.</li>
                    <li><strong>Dress in layers.</strong> You will warm up fast. Start warm but be ready to shed a layer at lunch.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="col-md-6 mb-4">
        <div class="card h-100">
            <div class="card-header bg-primary text-white">
                <h5 class="mb-0">Intermediate Tips</h5>
            </div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Carve your turns.</strong> Focus on edging, tilt the skis onto their edges and let them arc through the turn rather than skidding.</li>
                    <li><strong>Use your poles.</strong> Plant your pole at the start of each turn to help with rhythm and timing.</li>
                    <li><strong>Ski the whole mountain.</strong> Don't always take the same groomer. Try different terrain to develop versatility.</li>
                    <li><strong>Ski the moguls slowly at first.</strong> Pick a line and absorb each bump through your knees, keep your upper body quiet.</li>
                    <li><strong>Go first run.</strong> Hit the mountain early for freshly groomed runs before crowds and chop set in.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="col-md-6 mb-4">
        <div class="card h-100">
            <div class="card-header bg-dark text-white">
                <h5 class="mb-0">Advanced Tips</h5>
            </div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Vary your radius.</strong> Practice both short quick turns and long GS-style arcing turns. Versatility makes you a stronger skier.</li>
                    <li><strong>Powder technique.</strong> In deep snow, keep your weight centered or slightly back, turn with your whole body, and let the ski tips float.</li>
                    <li><strong>Stay athletic.</strong> Keep knees flexed and ready to absorb variable terrain. A stiff stance causes falls on uneven snow.</li>
                    <li><strong>Video yourself.</strong> Record a few runs and watch them back, you will almost always spot something you did not notice while skiing.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="col-md-6 mb-4">
        <div class="card h-100">
            <div class="card-header bg-secondary text-white">
                <h5 class="mb-0">Safety on the Mountain</h5>
            </div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Always wear a helmet.</strong> No exceptions, regardless of skill level.</li>
                    <li><strong>Know the Skier Responsibility Code.</strong> Yield to skiers below you, they have the right of way.</li>
                    <li><strong>Stay in control.</strong> You must be able to stop or avoid other skiers and objects at all times.</li>
                    <li><strong>Check the trail map.</strong> Know which runs are within your ability before dropping in.</li>
                    <li><strong>Buddy system.</strong> Never ski alone in the backcountry or in low-visibility conditions.</li>
                    <li><strong>Stay hydrated.</strong> High altitude accelerates dehydration. Drink water throughout the day.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
